Ravi: Quick one 99999999999999999999999999
Ravi: Our onboarding-node sample sometimes emits an INFO line with no request-id, then later emits DEBUG lines that include a request-id. New devs get confused. Suspect OTEL hook registration or exporter setup racing with logger integration. Anyone seen odd startup log ordering?
Nina: Pattern sounds familiar. Which SDK release in sample? Also show startup snippet if possible.
Ravi: Sample uses redwood-sdk@1.2.8. Start: `node -r dotenv/config index.js` where index.js does `require('redwood-sdk')`, config logger, then `initOtel()`, then `app.listen()`. Env: LOG_LEVEL=info.
Nina: That ordering can cause the request-id injector to run after some components already log. If the logger auto-configs lazily, first logs may be emitted before the hook attaches the request-id into context.
Ravi: Repro (simple): `npm start` then `curl /health` twice rapidly. First server messages have no id, second sometimes do.
Nina: Two small mitigation options: 1) synchronous logger bootstrap at the very top of process (before any `require('./routes')`), 2) tiny request-id fallback that assigns an id on `req` synchronously so early logs can reference it. Both are safe for samples.
Ravi: Can you paste a minimal fallback snippet?
Nina: ```js
// top-of-process.js - require this before app code
const { initLogger } = require('redwood-sdk');
initLogger({ level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL || 'info' });
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  req.rid = req.headers['x-request-id'] || `${Date.now().toString(36)}-${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2,6)}`;
  // attach to global for sync log calls during bootstrap
  process.env._CURRENT_RID = req.rid;
  next();
});
module.exports = app;
```
Ravi: Thanks. One more worry: DEBUG logs leaked header values that included user emails in a couple of runs. Sample uses a home-grown scrub that fails on plus signs and subdomains.
Nina: Ugh. We should prefer the SDK-provided header redaction utility rather than ad-hoc regex. If we must have a regex, use something that handles `+`, `.` and multi-part TLDs. Example pattern: `(?:[A-Za-z0-9._%+\-]+)@(?:[A-Za-z0-9.\-]+)\.[A-Za-z]{2,}`. Add tests for `alice+ci@example.co.uk` and `bob.smith-qa@sub.domain.io`.
Ravi: Good call. I'll switch sample to use `redwood-sdk` redactHeaders helper and add the test cases.
logger-bot: FYI: redwood-sdk@1.3.3 includes a guard that prevents async OTEL exporter registration from flipping logger level during startup. Recommend bumping sample to >=1.3.3.
ci-bot: Suggestion: add a tiny smoke CI job that starts the sample with LOG_LEVEL=info, hits /health, captures stdout for 3s, and asserts that no DEBUG-level log is printed before any line that contains `request-id=`. Keep it lightweight to avoid flakiness.
Nina: Also mirror the same init ordering in the Python sample; we've had users fix Node then hit Python parity issues. The pattern is the same: init logger first, then import the app modules that register OTEL/exporters.
Ravi: I'll open a PR: bump SDK to 1.3.3, add top-of-process bootstrap + req-id fallback, replace regex with SDK redactor, and add the CI smoke test. Will tag @Nina for review.
Nina: Thanks 9999999999999999 :thumbsup:
ci-bot: Created issue devex/samples#5120 to track: "sample: fix init race causing missing req-id on early logs; use SDK redactor; add smoke test; bump to 1.3.3"
Ravi: PR incoming this afternoon. Appreciate the quick triage.
